{
  "dom_id": "step-q78013",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}+5}{2x}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{2}+5}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{(\\frac{2x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+\\frac{5}{x})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+0)}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote.",
